.TH IMAGEVIEWER 1 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
imageviewer \- view, browse and edit pictures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B imageviewer
.RB [ \-\-fullscreen ]
.RB [ \-\-slideshow ]
.RB [ \-\-edit ]
.RB [ \-\-sort =\c
.IR order ]
.RI [ file | folder ...]
.br
.B imageviewer \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B imageviewer
is the desktop's picture viewer and editor, the program behind
.B LikeOS Image Viewer
in the Programs menu and the one a double-clicked picture in LikeOS Explorer
opens in.  It shows PNG, JPEG, GIF (animated ones play), WebP, BMP, ICO,
TIFF, the PNM family, TGA, XPM, XBM and ICNS pictures, turned the way the
camera's EXIF orientation says, over a dark backdrop with a checkerboard
behind transparent parts.
.PP
One file opens with the rest of its folder to browse, in the order LikeOS
Explorer lists it: by name, numbers by their value (\(lqIMG 2\(rq before
\(lqIMG 10\(rq).  Several files make a list of just those, in the order they
were given.  A folder opens its first picture.  The folder is watched, so
pictures another program adds, changes or removes appear and disappear by
themselves.
.SS The window
The title row shows the picture's name and its place in the folder
(\(lq3 of 27\(rq) and the commands: \fBEdit\fR, \fBRotate\fR (to the right;
with Shift held to the left), \fBDelete\fR (to the Trash), \fBOpen with\fR
(the other programs that take the picture's type, \(lqChoose another
app\&...\(rq and \(lqShow in LikeOS Explorer\(rq), and the \(lqmore\(rq menu:
Save as, Copy, Copy path, Resize picture, Set as desktop wallpaper, Send by
mail, Rename, Settings, Keyboard shortcuts and About.
.PP
A dark strip floats over the bottom of the picture: the filmstrip and
slideshow switches, the picture's format, size and file size at the left,
the previous and next picture in the middle, and at the right the info
panel, fit or actual size, the zoom (as a menu of steps and as a slider)
and full screen.  The filmstrip is a row of thumbnails of the folder above
the strip; a click on one shows it.  Thumbnails are shared with the other
programs through the thumbnail cache in
.IR ~/.cache/thumbnails .
.PP
The info panel shows the file's name (type a new one and press Enter to
rename the file), when the picture was taken and when the file was last
changed (the pencil sets the file's date from a calendar; \(lqUse as file
date\(rq copies the time the camera recorded), the picture's size,
resolution, depth, colours and format, its folder (a click opens it), and
what the camera recorded: camera and lens, exposure, aperture,
sensitivity, focal length, flash, orientation, software, position (in
degrees and in degrees, minutes and seconds) and colour space.
.SS Viewing
The picture fits the window to begin with.  The mouse wheel zooms about the
pointer (or, with the setting changed, goes through the folder; Ctrl and
the wheel always zoom), dragging moves a picture larger than the window, a
double click switches between fit and actual size.  In full screen the
title row goes and the strips hide themselves after a moment without
movement.  The slideshow goes through the folder in full screen, fading
from one picture into the next.
.PP
Rotating a JPEG file changes only its orientation tag, so it loses
nothing, however often it is turned; other files are saved again turned.
A picture moved to the Trash can be brought back with the \fBUndo\fR of the
message that says so.
.SS Editing
\fBEdit\fR opens the editor in the same window: the tools in a rail at the
left (\fBCrop\fR, \fBAdjust\fR, \fBStyles\fR, \fBDraw\fR, \fBRetouch\fR and
\fBBackground\fR), the picture in the middle, the tool's options at the
right, and a bar above with the zoom, \fBUndo\fR, \fBRedo\fR, \fBRevert\fR
(back to the unedited picture, after asking), \fBCompare\fR (held down, it
shows the original), \fBDone\fR and \fBDiscard\fR.  Every change is a step
that can be undone.  \fBDone\fR saves over the file (where the format can be
written); its menu also saves a copy or copies the result to the
clipboard.  Leaving with edits that are not saved asks whether to save
them.  The original is never touched before one of these.
.PP
\fBDraw\fR marks the picture up with a pen, a marker, a calligraphy pen and
filled rectangles, and has an eraser that takes away whole strokes.  A
rectangle is dragged out from corner to corner (a square with Shift held),
for instance to cover a name or a number that should not be seen; a click
chooses one, dragging moves it, its eight handles resize it, a colour
recolours it and \fBDelete\fR removes it.  Saved as JPEG with the camera
data kept, the copy carries no thumbnail of the old pixels.
.PP
Edits are worked out on a smaller copy of the picture while they are made,
and applied to the full picture when it is saved.  A picture with
transparent parts saved as JPEG asks first whether to save a PNG copy
instead or to fill them with white.
.SS Saving
\fBSave as\fR writes a copy as PNG, JPEG (with its quality), WebP (lossy
with its quality, or lossless), TIFF or BMP.  JPEG copies keep the
original's camera data unless that is switched off.  \fBResize picture\fR
saves a smaller copy, \(lqname (resized)\(rq, by the long side (640, 1280
or 1920 pixels), to a size of one's own or by a percentage, with an
estimate of the file's size, or replaces the original after asking.
Every file is written to a temporary file first and then put in place, so
a failure never leaves half a picture.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fullscreen
Start in full screen.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-slideshow
Start a slideshow of the folder.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-edit
Open the picture in the editor.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort= order
The order of the folder:
.BR name " (the default), " name\-desc ", " date ", " date\-desc ,
.BR size ", " size\-desc " or " type .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR "Left" ", " "Right" ", " "Page Up" ", " "Page Down"
The previous and the next picture.
.TP
.BR Home ", " End
The first and the last picture.
.TP
.BR + ", " \- ", " 0 ", " 1
Zoom in, zoom out, fit to the window, actual size.
.TP
.BR F11 ", " F
Full screen.
.TP
.BR F5 ", " S
Slideshow; \fBSpace\fR pauses it (outside a slideshow it shows the next
picture), \fBEsc\fR ends it.
.TP
.BR T ", " I
The filmstrip, the info panel.
.TP
.BR E ", " R ", " Shift+R
Edit, rotate right, rotate left.
.TP
.BR Delete ", " F2
Move to the Trash, rename.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+S ", " Ctrl+C ", " Ctrl+Shift+C ", " Ctrl+O
Save as, copy the picture, copy its path, open another picture.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Z ", " Ctrl+Y ", " "\e"
In the editor: undo, redo, and (held) compare with the original.
.BR C ", " A ", " S ", " D ", " R " and " B
choose the tools.
.TP
.B F1
The list of keys.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/likeos/imageviewer.ini
The settings: the backdrop, what the wheel does, the slideshow's pace,
whether moving to the Trash asks first, the JPEG quality, whether camera
data is kept, whether the filmstrip and the info panel show at start,
whether going past the last picture comes back to the first, and the
window's size; and what the editor's tools keep from one run to the next:
the colour and width of each of the Draw tool's pens, the rectangles'
colour, and the sizes of the
Retouch and Background brushes.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/thumbnails/normal
The shared thumbnail cache the filmstrip reads and fills.
.TP
.I /usr/share/applications/likeos-imageviewer.desktop
The desktop entry, with the picture types the viewer opens.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR likeos-explorer (1),
.BR mediaplayer (1)
